# `ExampleScanTest` compares `golden/example-declarations.json` byte for byte against what
# `DeclarationsJson.write` emits, and one of the two cases doing so - `two checkouts produce
# byte-identical json` - is a determinism assertion whose whole subject is the bytes. The writer
# emits LF and only LF, so a checkout that translates the golden makes the comparison fail on a
# difference nobody introduced.
#
# That is not hypothetical. Issue #176: this repository has no root `.gitattributes` and Git for
# Windows checks out with `core.autocrlf=true`, so `build (windows-latest)` had been failing both
# cases on a golden that arrives with `CR=169 LF=169` against output the writer emits with no
# carriage return at all. In the issue's own words the rendered assertion "looks identical on
# both sides, which is the tell", and it was settled by counting the bytes in the uploaded
# test-report artefact rather than by reading the diff.
#
# `-text` rather than `text eol=lf`, and rather than a repository-wide rule. `-text` says "never
# translate this in either direction", which is what a byte comparison needs, and it is what
# `udea-diagnostics/.gitattributes` and `.github/conformance/.gitattributes` (issue #171) already
# say for the same reason. A repository-wide `* text=auto eol=lf` would additionally rewrite
# `example/`'s asset corpus, which is committed CRLF on purpose - see the KDoc on
# `UdeaDeclarationScanner.normalizeLineEndings`, which found that the whole corpus is CRLF and
# that pass 1 is wrong on it without normalising.
#
# The fence that goes with this lives in `GoldenResource`, so that a copy translated by something
# other than a checkout - an archive, an editor, a working tree that predates this file - fails
# by name instead of failing as an equality diff of two identical-looking blocks.
src/test/resources/golden/*.json -text
